      "id": "bochner-inversion-and-plancherel-on-lca-groups-examples",
      "location": "First paragraph, finite-group normalization sentence (lines 18–20)",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The page says “with conversion factor |G|^{-1/2} to the unitary discrete Fourier transform.” The in-scope finite-group example gives, for N=|G|, the probability-Haar transform as N^{-1} times the raw sum and the unitary transform as N^{-1/2} times that sum, hence U f=N^{1/2} f̂ for the same input. N^{-1/2} is the coefficient in the unitary sums, not the output conversion from f̂.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "70f04673dc3fa52a26211c4cc3a2b466cda086b22e66763a10a6c2693e670d17"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:26:2",
      "id": "bochner-inversion-and-plancherel-on-lca-groups-examples",
      "location": "Second paragraph, character finite-test-matrix clause (lines 22–24)",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The page says “its finite test matrices are rank-one positive semidefinite.” The in-scope definition allows every n≥0 and specifies that the n=0 sum is empty and equals 0; the empty test matrix has rank 0. The in-scope character example correctly states rank one only for nonempty families and rank zero for the empty family.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "70f04673dc3fa52a26211c4cc3a2b466cda086b22e66763a10a6c2693e670d17"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:26:3",
      "id": "bochner-inversion-and-plancherel-on-lca-groups-examples",
      "location": "Final sentences about point modifications and everywhere inversion (lines 30–34)",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The page says “changing an L1 function at a single point preserves its class” and that inversion “is never an everywhere statement for an arbitrary representative.” The in-scope counterexample assumes G is nondiscrete and proves singleton Haar-nullity under that hypothesis. On a finite discrete group, singletons have positive Haar mass, so changing one point changes the class; the in-scope finite-group example also proves inversion at every point. The page summary needs the nondiscreteness hypothesis.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "70f04673dc3fa52a26211c4cc3a2b466cda086b22e66763a10a6c2693e670d17"
    }
  ],
